item.content:encoded:
**Claude Code hooks that crash used to fail open — the action they guarded went through anyway. `onFailure: "block"` closes that gap for `command` and `http` hooks.**

Your secret-scan hook runs `detect-secrets` on every `git commit`. The hook crashes because `detect-secrets` isn't installed in the CI container. Old behavior: commit goes through. With `onFailure: "block"`: commit is blocked.

This is the fail-closed vs. fail-open distinction that matters for any hook used as a security gate. Before v2.1.295 (October 8), a hook that failed to *run* — crashed, missing binary, network error on an HTTP hook — defaulted to letting the action through. That creates a bypass path for every error condition the hook doesn't anticipate.

## Wiring it up

Add `"onFailure": "block"` to any hook that must not silently pass on error:

```json
{
  "hooks": {
    "PreToolUse": [
      {
        "matcher": "Bash",
        "hooks": [
          {
            "type": "command",
            "command": "./hooks/secret-scan.sh",
            "onFailure": "block"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

For HTTP hooks, the same key works — a hook that can't reach its endpoint will block the action.

## What counts as a failure

`onFailure` applies to *failures to execute*: the process can't start, the binary is missing, the network is unreachable. It does **not** change how intentional non-zero exits work — exit 2 still blocks explicitly, exit 0 still passes. An HTTP hook returning 4xx or 5xx counts as a failure and will block.

## The trade-off

Any hook outage now blocks the action it guards. For a secret-scan or destructive-command gate, that's correct. For a logging-only or notification hook, it isn't — keep the default (fail open) for non-gate hooks so a transient crash doesn't halt legitimate work.

`onFailure: "block"` is the right default for every hook that doubles as a security gate. Leave it off for observability hooks where availability matters more than strict enforcement.

**Sources:** [Claude Code changelog v2.1.295 — code.claude.com](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/changelog) · [Claude Code hooks docs — code.claude.com](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks)

**Claude Code's interactive permission dialog blocks indefinitely in a headless pipeline — `--permission-prompt-tool` replaces it with a call to your own MCP tool that can approve, deny, or log each request programmatically.**

When you run Claude Code with `--print` in CI or a scheduled task, every permission prompt that isn't pre-approved in `settings.json` causes the process to hang. The standard escape is `--dangerouslySkipPermissions`, which bypasses all checks. `--permission-prompt-tool` is the third option: wire a custom MCP tool that receives each permission request and returns an explicit decision, so you get automation without blanket trust.

**What you'll be able to do after this:** auto-approve read-only tools, block destructive command patterns, and log every Claude permission decision to an audit trail — without touching `--dangerouslySkipPermissions`.

## The flag

```bash
claude --print \
  --mcp-config .mcp-permissions.json \
  --permission-prompt-tool mcp__permissions__handle \
  "Run the test suite and fix any failing tests"
```

`--permission-prompt-tool` only works with `--print`. Pass the full MCP tool name in `namespace__server__tool` format.

## What your handler receives

```json
{
  "tool_name": "Bash",
  "tool_input": {
    "command": "rm -rf ./dist",
    "description": "Clean build output"
  }
}
```

## What it must return

```json
{ "behavior": "allow" }

{ "behavior": "deny", "message": "Destructive rm blocked by CI policy" }

{
  "behavior": "allow",
  "updatedInput": { "command": "rm -rf ./dist --preserve-root" }
}
```

Set `updatedInput` when you want to modify the tool's input before Claude proceeds — Claude sees the updated version as if it had requested it originally.

## Minimal MCP handler (Python, stdio)

```python
import json, sys, asyncio
from mcp.server import Server
from mcp.server.stdio import stdio_server
from mcp import types

app = Server("permissions")

SAFE_TOOLS = {"Read", "Grep", "Glob"}
ALWAYS_DENY = {"mcp__github__delete_file"}
DANGEROUS_PATTERNS = ["rm -rf /", "DROP TABLE", "; curl | bash"]

@app.call_tool()
async def handle(name: str, arguments: dict) -> list[types.TextContent]:
    tool_name = arguments.get("tool_name", "")
    tool_input = arguments.get("tool_input", {})

    # Audit log every request
    print(f"PERM: {tool_name} | {json.dumps(tool_input)[:100]}", file=sys.stderr)

    if tool_name in ALWAYS_DENY:
        result = {"behavior": "deny", "message": f"{tool_name} blocked by policy"}
    elif tool_name in SAFE_TOOLS:
        result = {"behavior": "allow"}
    elif tool_name == "Bash":
        command = tool_input.get("command", "")
        if any(p in command for p in DANGEROUS_PATTERNS):
            result = {"behavior": "deny", "message": "Destructive pattern blocked"}
        else:
            result = {"behavior": "allow"}
    else:
        result = {"behavior": "allow"}

    return [types.TextContent(type="text", text=json.dumps(result))]

asyncio.run(stdio_server(app))
```

## MCP config

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "permissions": {
      "command": "python3",
      "args": ["scripts/permission_handler.py"]
    }
  }
}
```

## When to use this vs. other approaches

| Approach | Right for |
|---|---|
| `allowedTools` in `settings.json` | Static pre-approved list; no dynamic logic needed |
| `--dangerouslySkipPermissions` | Fully sandboxed environments (Docker, disposable VMs) |
| `--permission-prompt-tool` | Dynamic approval; audit logs; Bash command inspection; Slack escalation |

The key advantage over a static allowlist: your handler sees the *inputs*, not just the tool name. A Bash handler can allow `pnpm test` and deny `rm -rf /tmp/important` — a distinction a name-only allowlist cannot make.

Full reference: [Claude Code SDK docs](https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/claude-code/sdk)

**Commit `.mcp.json` to your project root and every Claude Code session your team opens gets the same MCP servers — no per-machine `claude mcp add` required.**

Claude Code resolves MCP servers from three scopes: user-level (`~/.claude/mcp.json`), project-level (`.mcp.json` at the repo root), and session-level flags. Project-level is the only scope you can commit to version control and share across the team.

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "playwright": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@playwright/mcp@latest"],
      "type": "stdio"
    },
    "github": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@modelcontextprotocol/server-github"],
      "type": "stdio",
      "env": {
        "GITHUB_TOKEN": "${GITHUB_TOKEN}"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

**Key details:**

- **`${VAR}` syntax for secrets.** Environment variables expand at runtime so a literal API token never lands in git. Document which variables to export in your `.env.example`.
- **First-use approval gate.** When a teammate opens the repo for the first time, Claude Code prompts them to approve the project-scoped servers before they load. This is an intentional security checkpoint — do not use `--allowedTools` to bypass it.
- **Scope precedence.** If a user-scoped server has the same name, the project-scoped entry wins. This lets the repo pin a specific MCP server version while individuals keep personal defaults.

**When to commit `.mcp.json`:** do it when the team needs a shared tool — a Playwright server for browser testing, an internal data-access server, a project-specific API wrapper. Avoid committing servers that only make sense for one person, or servers requiring user-specific credentials baked in beyond `${VAR}` expansion.

One limit: each teammate still needs to have the server's runtime available locally (e.g., Node.js installed for `npx` commands). The committed config handles discovery; it does not handle dependencies.

**Sources:** [MCP in Claude Code — Anthropic Docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/mcp) · [Claude Code MCP Server Configuration Guide — Glama](https://glama.ai/mcp/servers/@marc-shade/universal-ai-chat/blob/3d65574a888705c4f462b8331a186f0721e0e115/docs/claude-code-mcp-docs.md)

**What you'll be able to do after this:** Set up a Style Dictionary build step so Claude Code uses your color scale, type scale, and spacing — not generic Tailwind defaults — when generating components.

**Three things to know first:**
- Style Dictionary reads your tokens in the W3C DTCG JSON format and emits CSS custom properties (or Tailwind config, or both), keeping component generation locked to your system
- One line in CLAUDE.md tells every session where the compiled token file lives; without it, Claude Code guesses from context and falls back on defaults
- The DTCG Community Group format is still a draft (not a W3C Recommendation), but Figma, Tokens Studio, and Style Dictionary all speak it, making it the practical interop point today

**Setup (5 steps):**

1. Install: `npm install --save-dev style-dictionary`

2. Create `tokens/brand.tokens.json`:
```json
{
  "color": {
    "primary":    { "$type": "color",     "$value": "#3B82F6" },
    "text":       { "$type": "color",     "$value": "#111827" },
    "surface":    { "$type": "color",     "$value": "#F9FAFB" }
  },
  "spacing": {
    "4":  { "$type": "dimension", "$value": "1rem" },
    "8":  { "$type": "dimension", "$value": "2rem" }
  },
  "fontFamily": {
    "base": { "$type": "fontFamily", "$value": "Inter, system-ui, sans-serif" }
  }
}
```

3. Create `style-dictionary.config.json`:
```json
{
  "source": ["tokens/**/*.tokens.json"],
  "platforms": {
    "css": {
      "transformGroup": "css",
      "prefix": "ds",
      "buildPath": "src/styles/",
      "files": [{ "destination": "tokens.css", "format": "css/variables" }]
    }
  }
}
```

4. Run `npx style-dictionary build` → generates `src/styles/tokens.css` with `--ds-color-primary: #3B82F6;`, `--ds-spacing-4: 1rem;`, etc.

5. Add to CLAUDE.md:
```
Design tokens: src/styles/tokens.css
Use these CSS custom properties for all component colors, spacing, and typography.
Do not use raw hex values or Tailwind's default color palette.
```

**One real limit:** Style Dictionary adds a build step. Without automation, `tokens.css` drifts from `tokens.json` when designs update. Add a prebuild script: `"prebuild": "style-dictionary build"` so the CSS regenerates before every deploy. If you export tokens from Figma, the loop is: Figma export → `tokens.json` → `npx style-dictionary build` → Claude Code reads `tokens.css`.

**Sources:** [Style Dictionary docs — styledictionary.com](https://styledictionary.com) · [Stop AI from Ignoring Your Design Tokens: DTCG + AGENTS.md Workflow — atomize.tools](https://atomize.tools/blog/figma-design-tokens-vibe-coding/) · [Design Tokens for AI Coding: CLAUDE.md Rules — techarion.com](https://techarion.com/blog/design-tokens-claude-md-rules-stop-generic-ai-ui)

**MCP has three server primitives — Tools, Resources, and Prompts. Building only Tools is like building a REST API with only POST routes.**

The [earlier post on MCP Tools](https://cloudcodetree.com/ai-news/2026-10-04-01-build-mcp-server-typescript-sdk-tools-zod-stdio) showed how to expose functions the model can call. Two more primitives handle different responsibilities:

- **Resources** — context data the host app injects into the model's context: files, database records, config payloads. Modeled as URIs, equivalent to a REST GET. Designed to be read-only with no side effects.
- **Prompts** — named, parameterized message templates the user (or the host app) selects before a conversation. Pre-fill a system instruction, set a workflow role, or wrap a multi-step task in a reusable invocation.

**Adding a Resource** (TypeScript SDK)

```typescript
import { McpServer, ResourceTemplate } from "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/mcp.js";
import { z } from "zod";

const server = new McpServer({ name: "docs-server", version: "1.0.0" });

// Static resource — same content every request
server.resource("app-config", "config://app/current", async (uri) => ({
  contents: [{ uri: uri.href, mimeType: "application/json",
    text: JSON.stringify({ maxRetries: 3, timeout: 30 }) }]
}));

// Templated resource — URI parameter resolved at read time
server.resource(
  "user-profile",
  new ResourceTemplate("users://{userId}/profile", { list: undefined }),
  async (uri, { userId }) => ({
    contents: [{ uri: uri.href, mimeType: "application/json",
      text: await fetchUserProfile(userId) }]
  })
);
```

Claude resolves `users://42/profile` when `resources/read` is called with that URI. The host app calls `resources/list` first to enumerate what's available.

**Adding a Prompt**

```typescript
server.prompt(
  "code-review",
  { language: z.string() },
  ({ language }) => ({
    messages: [{
      role: "user",
      content: {
        type: "text",
        text: `Review the following ${language} code for correctness, security, and style.
Flag anything that would fail a production review.`
      }
    }]
  })
);
```

The host surfaces `code-review` to the user. On selection, it pre-fills context before the conversation starts. Arguments validate through Zod the same as Tool inputs.

**When to reach for each primitive**

| Use case | Primitive |
|---|---|
| Call an external API, run a query, execute an action | Tool |
| Inject a file, config doc, or DB record as context | Resource |
| Pre-fill a multi-turn workflow with a reusable template | Prompt |

**One real limit**: Resources are polled, not pushed. A resource that changes frequently — live logs, real-time prices — won't push updates between model turns. The app re-reads it on each turn. If you need live data streaming into the conversation, use a Tool with a streaming or websocket response instead.

**Sources:** [MCP TypeScript SDK v2 docs](https://ts.sdk.modelcontextprotocol.io/v2/) · [MCP spec — Resources](https://spec.modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/basic/resources/) · [MCP spec — Prompts](https://spec.modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/basic/prompts/) · [SDK runnable examples — simpleStreamableHttp server](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/tree/main/src/examples/server)

**AI design tools produce components that routinely fail WCAG 2.1 AA — @axe-core/playwright catches the structural failures in ~20 lines of test code, so Claude Code can fix them in the same PR.**

**What you'll be able to do after this:** Wire an accessibility audit to your Playwright test suite, run it in CI, and get a structured list of violations your Claude Code session can fix before the component ships.

**Why generated components fail**

Claude Design, v0, and similar tools optimize for visual output. They have no feedback loop for WCAG compliance — what renders correctly may have no accessible label, poor contrast, or a missing landmark. The same failures top the WebAIM Million every year: missing `alt` text, low contrast ratios, empty links and buttons, unlabeled form inputs. Generating UI from a prompt reliably produces several of them; prompting for accessibility helps but doesn't reliably eliminate them.

**The audit stack: @axe-core/playwright**

`@axe-core/playwright` is Deque's official wrapper — the engine behind browser DevTools' Accessibility panel, exposed as a Playwright test step:

```bash
npm install -D @axe-core/playwright
```

```ts
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';
import AxeBuilder from '@axe-core/playwright';

test('generated component — accessibility', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/preview/my-component');
  const results = await new AxeBuilder({ page })
    .withTags(['wcag2a', 'wcag2aa'])
    .analyze();
  expect(results.violations).toHaveLength(0);
});
```

When violations exist, the output lists each rule (`color-contrast`, `label`, `button-name`), the element, and the fix. Paste the `violations` array into your Claude Code session — it can trace each element back to its source file and apply the fix.

**Scoping and triage**

`.include('#component-root')` narrows the scan to a single component. `.disableRules(['color-contrast'])` lets you defer categories you'll address separately. `.withTags(['wcag2aa'])` targets the AA level most teams treat as a baseline.

**CI integration:** add the test to the same pipeline that runs before merge. A failing accessibility test blocks the PR; the violation output in the logs gives Claude Code the exact element and rule to fix.

**The 30% ceiling**

Automated scans catch roughly 30% of WCAG issues — color contrast, missing labels, and landmark structure are well-covered. They miss keyboard traps (a modal you can tab into but not exit), dynamic focus management, and screen reader announcement timing. Tab through the component manually after the automated gate passes; axe confirms the structure, not the experience.

**Sources:** [@axe-core/playwright — Deque / npm](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@axe-core/playwright) · [Accessibility Testing with Playwright — Infinum](https://infinum.com/?p=19265085) · [Playwright Accessibility Testing Guide — TestMu AI](https://www.testmuai.com/blog/playwright-web-accessibility-tests/)

**Set `output_config={"format": {"type": "json", "json_schema": {...}}}` and Claude's constrained decoding prevents any non-schema response at the sampling level — no retry loop needed.**

The traditional approach — "respond in JSON like this:" prompting — retries when Claude wraps the JSON in prose or produces a schema violation. The `tool_choice` force pattern guarantees a tool call but changes how Claude reasons about ambiguous input. `output_config.format` is a third path: **constrained decoding during generation**. Only tokens that keep the response valid against your schema can be sampled.

```python
import anthropic, json

client = anthropic.Anthropic()
response = client.messages.create(
    model="claude-sonnet-4-6",
    max_tokens=1024,
    output_config={
        "format": {
            "type": "json",
            "json_schema": {
                "name": "EntityExtraction",
                "schema": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "entities": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}},
                        "sentiment": {"type": "string", "enum": ["positive", "negative", "neutral"]},
                        "summary": {"type": "string"}
                    },
                    "required": ["entities", "sentiment", "summary"],
                    "additionalProperties": False
                }
            }
        }
    },
    messages=[{"role": "user", "content": "Analyze: 'Anthropic released a new prompting guide today.'"}]
)
# response.content[0].text is always valid JSON — parse directly, no try/except needed
data = json.loads(response.content[0].text)
```

**Schema rule:** every object in the schema must set `"additionalProperties": False` (Python) / `false` (JSON Schema), or the API returns a 400.

**SDK shortcut:** the Python SDK's `client.messages.parse()` accepts a Pydantic model directly — the SDK generates the schema and deserializes the response into a model instance.

**What this doesn't fix:** constrained decoding guarantees the *shape* is right, not the *content*. It prevents `{"sentiment": "unknown"}` (not in the enum), but not `{"sentiment": "positive"}` on a text that's actually negative. Validate semantic correctness in your application.

**Limits to know:**
- Available on Claude Opus 4.8, Mythos Preview, Opus 4.7, 4.6, Sonnet 4.6, 4.5, Opus 4.5, Haiku 4.5 — not every Claude model
- On AWS Bedrock: `output_config` cannot be combined with the citations feature (returns 400)
- The old `output_format` parameter still works during transition, then deprecated — migrate now
- Assistant-turn prefill is no longer supported on Opus 4.6+ models; use structured outputs instead

**Sources:** [Structured outputs — Anthropic docs](https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/structured-outputs) · [Structured outputs vs tool use vs prefills in 2026 — DEV community](https://dev.to/pavelespitia/structured-outputs-vs-tool-use-vs-prefills-getting-json-out-of-claude-in-2026-5fi0) · [Anthropic structured output example — Deno](https://docs.deno.com/examples/anthropic_structured_output/)

**Your Cloudflare Worker is one binding away from calling open models at the edge — no separate inference server, no API key management beyond your existing CF token.**

**What you'll be able to do after this:** call Llama, Mistral, Qwen, or Gemma from any Worker function; route inference through an OpenAI-compatible endpoint from any language; add streaming LLM responses to an existing API without a separate backend.

**Three things to understand before you start:**
- The `[ai]` binding in `wrangler.toml` exposes `env.AI.run(modelId, input)` from inside your handler — it works like a local async function; Cloudflare routes it to GPU hardware on the nearest edge node.
- Model IDs follow `@cf/<vendor>/<model>` naming (e.g. `@cf/meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct-fast`). Run `npx wrangler ai models list` for the current catalog — IDs rotate and old ones break without warning.
- Cloudflare also exposes an OpenAI-compatible endpoint at `https://api.cloudflare.com/client/v4/accounts/{ACCOUNT_ID}/ai/v1` — swap `base_url` and use your CF API token, and any OpenAI SDK client works unchanged.

**Walk-through (Workers + Wrangler):**

```bash
npm create cloudflare@latest my-ai-worker
# select: Worker → TypeScript → no Git
cd my-ai-worker
```

In `wrangler.toml`, add:
```toml
[ai]
binding = "AI"
```

In `src/index.ts`:
```typescript
interface Env { AI: Ai }

export default {
  async fetch(req: Request, env: Env): Promise<Response> {
    const result = await env.AI.run(
      "@cf/meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct-fast",
      {
        messages: [
          { role: "system", content: "You are a helpful assistant." },
          { role: "user",   content: await req.text() }
        ]
      }
    );
    return Response.json(result);
  }
} satisfies ExportedHandler<Env>;
```

Test locally with `wrangler dev` (you'll be prompted to log in). Deploy with `wrangler deploy`.

**Streaming:** pass `stream: true` in the run options and pipe the resulting `ReadableStream` directly with `Content-Type: text/event-stream`.

**Limits to know before you ship:**
- All Workers AI models run at 4-bit or 8-bit quantization. Full-precision inference isn't available on this platform.
- The free plan includes a daily neuron quota (Cloudflare's billing unit per Cloudflare's pricing docs); usage beyond that is billed. Check the dashboard for current rates — they change.
- Model IDs rotate. Pin a specific ID in your Worker, test on each deploy, and monitor for 404s in production.
- No persistent sessions: each request cold-starts if the edge node hasn't warmed recently on low-traffic routes.
- Worker inputs are processed on Cloudflare's shared infrastructure. Review Cloudflare's data handling terms before routing confidential data through this path.

**Sources:** [Workers AI — Workers + Wrangler get started (Cloudflare Docs)](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers-ai/get-started/workers-wrangler/) · [Workers AI models catalog (Cloudflare Docs)](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers-ai/models/) · [REST API quickstart (Cloudflare Docs)](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers-ai/get-started/rest-api/) · [Workers AI LLM inference guide — noqta.tn](https://noqta.tn/en/tutorials/cloudflare-workers-ai-llm-inference-typescript-2026)

**`tool_choice: {type: 'tool', name: X}` guarantees structured output — but in Sonnet 5.5 it also suppresses the model's ability to flag ambiguous input. A two-tool pattern with `type: 'any'` and a `clarify` sentinel gives you both.**

The Claude Messages API exposes three `tool_choice` modes:

```json
// Mode 1: Claude decides whether to call a tool
{ "type": "auto" }

// Mode 2: Claude must call *some* tool — it picks which
{ "type": "any" }

// Mode 3: Claude must call exactly *this* tool
{ "type": "tool", "name": "extract_order" }
```

**When `type: 'tool'` is right.** High-volume, predictable inputs where you control the content — structured API responses, your own database records, fixed document templates. Output is always valid JSON, no prompt engineering required.

**When it breaks.** Sonnet 5.5 was trained to reason through ambiguity. Forcing a specific tool call works against that: the model fills in schema fields rather than pausing on uncertain inputs. The Anthropic migration guide notes that forced single-tool calls can lead to less thoughtful, mechanical output in Sonnet 5.5 — you get silently wrong JSON instead of a legible failure or a clarifying question.

**The fix: `type: 'any'` with a sentinel.** Add a second minimal tool alongside your extraction tool:

```python
import anthropic

client = anthropic.Anthropic()

tools = [
    {
        "name": "extract_order",
        "description": "Extract structured order details from the user's message.",
        "input_schema": {
            "type": "object",
            "properties": {
                "item": {"type": "string"},
                "quantity": {"type": "integer"},
                "delivery_date": {"type": "string", "format": "date"}
            },
            "required": ["item", "quantity"]
        }
    },
    {
        "name": "clarify",
        "description": "Ask one clarifying question when the input is ambiguous or missing required fields.",
        "input_schema": {
            "type": "object",
            "properties": {"question": {"type": "string"}},
            "required": ["question"]
        }
    }
]

response = client.messages.create(
    model="claude-sonnet-5-5",
    max_tokens=1024,
    tools=tools,
    tool_choice={"type": "any"},   # must call some tool; Claude picks which
    messages=[{"role": "user", "content": user_input}]
)

tool_call = response.content[0]
if tool_call.name == "clarify":
    # Return the question to the user and loop
    follow_up = tool_call.input["question"]
elif tool_call.name == "extract_order":
    order = tool_call.input  # guaranteed valid JSON
```

`type: 'any'` ensures Claude always calls a tool (no free-text fallback), but lets it route to `clarify` when the input is ambiguous. Handle the `clarify` branch by returning the question and re-running with the user's answer. Structured output semantics hold either way.

**One real limit:** `type: 'any'` with two tools adds a small reasoning cost relative to `type: 'tool'`. For high-volume pipelines with stable, predictable inputs, forced tool calls are still the right default. Reserve the sentinel pattern for user-facing agents and workflows that consume variable real-world text.

**Sources:** [Tool use overview — tool_choice — Anthropic Docs](https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/tool-use/overview) · [Claude Sonnet 5.5 migration guide — Anthropic Docs](https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/about-claude/models/migration-guide)

**SubagentStop's `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext` (Claude Code 2.1.163+) lets your hook inject a string into the lead's context window the instant a subagent finishes — turn a binary pass/block gate into an audit log with details, without a second hook or a temp file.**

A SubagentStop hook fires between a subagent's last message and the lead seeing its summary. Before 2.1.163, your options were `exit 0` (let it pass) or `exit 2` (block and retry). Now you can return `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext` — a string the lead sees injected into its context right after the subagent's turn.

This matters for multi-subagent workflows where the lead needs to act on *what* a subagent found, not just whether it passed. Previously the standard workaround was writing state to a temp file and picking it up in a separate UserPromptSubmit hook; `additionalContext` collapses that into one script.

## Pattern: gate + audit trail

```python
# .claude/hooks/gate_subagent.py
import sys, json, subprocess

payload = json.loads(sys.stdin.read())

result = subprocess.run(
    ["pnpm", "test", "--reporter=compact"],
    capture_output=True, text=True
)

if result.returncode != 0:
    sys.stderr.write(json.dumps({
        "decision": "block",
        "reason": result.stdout[-500:]
    }))
    sys.exit(2)   # block: subagent is sent back to fix it

sha = subprocess.check_output(
    ["git", "rev-parse", "--short", "HEAD"]
).decode().strip()

summary_line = result.stdout.strip().splitlines()[-1] if result.stdout.strip() else "all passed"
print(json.dumps({
    "continue": True,
    "hookSpecificOutput": {
        "additionalContext": f"tests passed at {sha}: {summary_line}"
    }
}))
```

Wire it in `.claude/settings.json`:

```json
{
  "hooks": {
    "SubagentStop": [{
      "matcher": "**",
      "hooks": [{"type": "command", "command": "python3 .claude/hooks/gate_subagent.py"}]
    }]
  }
}
```

The lead sees your string injected as a system reminder immediately after the subagent's turn. In the transcript it appears as a hook-injected context block labelled with the hook type. The lead can reference it when planning its next action.

**Limits:** Hook timeout is 5 seconds. If your validation (build + test) takes longer, spawn it in a background process, write the result to a cache file, and have the hook return the cached result on subsequent fires. `additionalContext` is a flat string — no structured fields. Avoid dumping large logs into it since it costs tokens in the lead's context window verbatim.

**Sources:** [Hooks reference — Claude Code Docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks) · [SubagentStop patterns in production — pixelmojo](https://www.pixelmojo.io/blogs/claude-code-hooks-production-quality-ci-cd-patterns) · [claude-code-hooks-mastery examples — disler GitHub](https://github.com/disler/claude-code-hooks-mastery)

**AI design tools — Claude Design, v0, Magic Patterns — emit viewport-based media queries. A component that looked right in the prototype breaks when you drop it into a sidebar or a dashboard grid cell, because the viewport is still wide. Container queries fix this with one property on the wrapper.**

## What you'll be able to do after this:

- Identify which generated CSS will break in complex layouts before you paste it into your codebase
- Convert viewport-based media queries to container queries in under five minutes per component
- Make generated components portable — they adapt to their parent, not the window size

## Why generated components break in context

Claude Design and v0 prototype at full canvas width. The responsive rules they emit assume the component fills the viewport:

```css
.card { flex-direction: column; }
@media (min-width: 768px) {
  .card { flex-direction: row; }
}
```

Paste this card into a 300px sidebar in a dashboard layout: the viewport is still 1280px wide, so `min-width: 768px` fires, and the card tries to lay out horizontally in a 300px slot. It overflows or collapses. The breakpoint in the code means "viewport is wide", but what you meant was "this component has room to spread out".

## The fix: `container-type: inline-size`

Container queries let an element respond to the size of its *containing block*, not the viewport.

**Step 1 — add containment context to the wrapper:**

```css
.card-wrapper {
  container-type: inline-size;
}
```

**Step 2 — replace the media query with a container query:**

```css
.card { flex-direction: column; }

@container (min-width: 500px) {
  .card { flex-direction: row; }
}
```

The threshold changes from "viewport ≥ 768px" to "this card's container ≥ 500px". Now the component works whether it's in a sidebar, a three-column grid, or a full-width layout — without any special-casing at the page level.

**The threshold number also changes:** your generated breakpoints were calibrated to viewport widths (~768px, ~1024px). A component container is almost always narrower. Pick a value proportional to the component's minimum comfortable width, not the original viewport breakpoint.

## Tailwind shortcut

If you generate with Tailwind, install the official plugin:

```bash
npm install @tailwindcss/container-queries
```

Then use container-aware variants: `@sm:flex-row`, `@lg:grid-cols-3`. Same concept; no raw CSS needed.

## Limits

Container queries require a containment context on an ancestor — you can't query the root `<html>` element (there's nothing above it to measure). Also: an element cannot query inside its own containment boundary, so deeply nested query chains can get confusing. Finally, `container-type: inline-size` triggers layout containment, which affects stacking contexts — test for z-index surprises.

**Browser support:** universally available since 2023. Your users have it.

**Read:** [Container queries in 2026: Powerful, but not a silver bullet — LogRocket Blog](https://blog.logrocket.com/container-queries-2026/)

**Sources:** [LogRocket Blog — Container queries 2026](https://blog.logrocket.com/container-queries-2026/) · [SitePoint — CSS Container Queries and Subgrid](https://www.sitepoint.com/css-container-queries-subgrid-component-layouts-2026/) · [MDN — CSS container queries](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/CSS_containment/Container_queries)

**An MCP server is a process that registers typed tools (functions with Zod schemas) that any MCP-compatible client can call — build one in under 30 minutes using the official TypeScript SDK.**

**What you'll be able to do after this:** connect a calendar, database, REST API, or internal tool to Claude (and any other MCP client) as a native tool the agent calls, not a prompt trick you maintain.

**Three things to know before you start:**
- The SDK is the standard implementation: 97 million monthly downloads, 13,000+ servers registered on npm and GitHub.
- Tools expose a name, a Zod schema for inputs, and an async handler — the client never sees your code, only the schema.
- stdio transport works for local use; HTTP/SSE transport is needed for remote or multi-client deployments.

**Walk-through (Node 20, TypeScript):**

1. Initialize and install:
```bash
npm init -y && npx tsc --init
npm install @modelcontextprotocol/sdk zod
```

2. Create `server.ts` and initialize `McpServer`:
```typescript
import { McpServer } from "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/mcp.js";
import { StdioServerTransport } from "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/stdio.js";
import { z } from "zod";

const server = new McpServer({ name: "my-tools", version: "1.0.0" });
```

3. Register a tool with a Zod schema:
```typescript
server.tool(
  "get-events",
  "Fetch calendar events for a given date (YYYY-MM-DD)",
  { date: z.string().describe("ISO date string, e.g. 2026-10-04") },
  async ({ date }) => {
    const events = await fetchCalendarEvents(date); // your API call here
    return { content: [{ type: "text", text: events.join("\n") }] };
  }
);
```

4. Connect and run:
```typescript
const transport = new StdioServerTransport();
await server.connect(transport);
```

5. Register in Claude Desktop's `claude_desktop_config.json`:
```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "my-tools": {
      "command": "node",
      "args": ["dist/server.js"],
      "env": { "MY_API_KEY": "${MY_API_KEY}" }
    }
  }
}
```

6. Test with MCP Inspector (`npx @modelcontextprotocol/inspector`) before wiring to a live client — it shows the handshake, tool list, and individual call results.

**Limits:** Tool descriptions are the only documentation the model has about your tool — vague descriptions produce inconsistent calls, so write them like docstrings. stdio transport works only for local single-client use; if you need the server running remotely or serving multiple clients, you'll need the streamable HTTP transport, which adds session management.

**Full hands-on video (40 min, free lab environment):** [MCP Tutorial: Build Your First MCP Server and Client from Scratch — KodeKloud](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RhTiAOGwbYE). Covers JSON-RPC 2.0 internals, both server and client side, and walks through the inspector flow.

**Sources:** [FreeCodeCamp MCP server handbook](https://www.freecodecamp.org/news/how-to-build-a-custom-mcp-server-with-typescript-a-handbook-for-developers/) · [MCP TypeScript SDK — modelcontextprotocol/sdk on GitHub](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/sdk) · [KodeKloud YouTube — MCP Tutorial](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RhTiAOGwbYE)
